## DESCRIPTION
## Determine a representation matrix of integration of polynomials.
## ENDDESCRIPTION


## DBsubject(Linear algebra)
## DBchapter(Linear transformations)
## DBsection(Associated matrices)
## Date(September 5, 2014)
## Institution(Fitchburg State University)
## Author(Peter Staab)
## KEYWORDS('lines')

# updated 2022.10.30

DOCUMENT();
loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

Context("Vector");

# Define all variables used in the problem statement and solution

$ans = Matrix([ [ 0, 0, 0 ], [ 1, 0, 0 ], [ 0, 0.5, 0 ], [ 0, 0, 1 / 3 ] ]);

BEGIN_PGML
Let [`f: \mathcal{P}_2 \rightarrow \mathcal{P}_3`] be the integration map applied to polynomials.  That is

[``f(a_0 + a_1 x + a_2 x^2) = a_0 x + \frac{a_1}{2} x^2 + \frac{a_2}{3} x^3 ``]

Use the standard basis to find the matrix representing this map.

[___]*{$ans}

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
In this case, we apply the map (integration) to each standard basis vector. 
In terms of vectors recall that we can write [`x^k`] as [`\vec{e}_{k+1}`]

[```
\begin{aligned}
f(1) & = x &&\text{therefore} & f(\vec{e}_1) &= \vec{e}_2 \\
f(x) & = \frac{x^2}{2} &&\text{therefore} & f(\vec{e}_2) &= \frac{1}{2}\vec{e}_2 \\
f(x^2) & = \frac{x^3}{3} &&\text{therefore} & f(\vec{e}_3) &= \frac{1}{3} \vec{e}_3 \\
\end{aligned}
```]
So the three output vectors form the matrix or 

[``` [$ans]```]
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
